## Summary

`scripts/version-and-commit.mjs` and `scripts/merge-changesets.mjs` **skip a changeset they cannot parse** (for example `'MyPackage': minr`), printing only `Warning: Could not parse version type from …`. The release then deletes *every* changeset file, including the skipped one, and ends green. The broken changeset's bump and its description are silently lost: a minor change is released as a patch and is missing from `CHANGELOG.md` and the GitHub release.

`scripts/validate-changeset.mjs` does not prevent this, because it only checks changesets that a PR **adds** (`status === 'A'`). A PR that edits a pending changeset, a direct push, or a release-PR branch (which skips the check) brings an unparseable changeset to `main` unchecked.

Code (template `main` at 4cc372e):

- [`processChangesets`](https://github.com/link-foundation/csharp-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/blob/4cc372ebf2075a0c948b46881143f7d66f0f0e7e/scripts/version-and-commit.mjs#L253-L292): `if (parsed) { parsedChangesets.push(…) }`; any failure only warns
- [`removeChangesetFiles`](https://github.com/link-foundation/csharp-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/blob/4cc372ebf2075a0c948b46881143f7d66f0f0e7e/scripts/version-and-commit.mjs#L346-L353): deletes every changeset, parsed or not
- [`merge-changesets.mjs` `main`](https://github.com/link-foundation/csharp-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/blob/4cc372ebf2075a0c948b46881143f7d66f0f0e7e/scripts/merge-changesets.mjs#L208-L223): fails only when *no* changeset parses
- [`parseAddedChangesets`](https://github.com/link-foundation/csharp-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/blob/4cc372ebf2075a0c948b46881143f7d66f0f0e7e/scripts/validate-changeset.mjs#L53-L70): only `A` entries are validated

## Reproduction

`repro-csharp-invalid-changeset.sh` clones the given revision with a bare `origin`. It replaces the pending changesets with a valid patch changeset and a minor one whose bump type has a typo, then runs `node scripts/version-and-commit.mjs --mode changeset` as the release job does:

```bash
repro-csharp-invalid-changeset.sh /path/to/template-clone HEAD
```

Output against 4cc372e (abridged):

```
Warning: Could not parse version type from .changeset/add-export.md
Bump types found: patch
Using highest: patch
...
Output: version_committed=true
Output: new_version=0.3.17
exit=0
== changesets left in .changeset
README.md
config.json
== newest CHANGELOG.md entry
## [0.3.17] - 2026-10-24

Fix a typo in the help text.

== does CHANGELOG.md mention the minor change?
0
```

The script needs only `git` and `node`. It lives in link-foundation/link-cli (issue 134, pull request 135) at `dev/log/issues/134/pulls/135/scripts/repro-csharp-invalid-changeset.sh`. The outputs are `analysis/template-csharp-invalid-changeset-repro.txt` (template) and `analysis/csharp-invalid-changeset-repro.txt` (link-cli before and after the fix).

## Workaround

Before merging, check every pending changeset by hand with `node scripts/validate-changeset.mjs` (outside a PR it validates all of them). After a release that went wrong, restore the deleted changeset from git and release the missing bump manually.

## Suggested fix

Fail instead of skipping, in both scripts:

```js
const parsedChangesets = [];
const invalid = [];
for (const file of files) {
  const filePath = join(CHANGESET_DIR, file);
  const parsed = parseChangeset(filePath);
  if (parsed) {
    parsedChangesets.push({ file, filePath, ...parsed });
  } else {
    invalid.push(file);
  }
}

// Every changeset is deleted after the release, so one that does not parse
// would lose its bump and its description on a green run.
if (invalid.length > 0) {
  console.error(
    `::error title=Invalid changeset::Cannot parse ${invalid.join(', ')} in ${CHANGESET_DIR}. ` +
      `How to fix: start it with ---, '${PACKAGE_NAME}': major|minor|patch, --- and a description.`
  );
  process.exit(1);
}
```

Tests worth adding (link-cli has both in `csharp/scripts/release-scripts.test.mjs`):

- `merge-changesets` exits 1 with `Invalid changeset` and keeps every file;
- `version-and-commit --mode changeset`, run against a bare remote, exits 1 and pushes neither a commit nor a tag.

Optionally, `validate-changeset.mjs` could also validate changesets a PR modifies (`M`), so that the error shows up on the PR instead of on `main`.

The js template is not affected: its `merge-changesets.mjs` leaves an unparseable file in place, and `changeset version` (`@changesets/parse`) rejects it with `invalid version type`.

## Summary

`scripts/check-web-archive.mjs` asks the Wayback Machine availability API **once** per broken link and reads **any failed lookup as "not archived"**: a 429, a 5xx, a dropped connection, the 10 s abort or an unreadable answer. The job then fails with

```
::error title=Broken link - No Web Archive fallback::Broken link detected: <url>
No archived version was found in the Wayback Machine.
How to fix:
  1. Find an updated URL for the same or equivalent content and replace the link.
  ...
```

That message is wrong whenever the archive was never actually asked, and the author is told to replace a link that may well have a snapshot. archive.org rate-limits shared CI addresses: on 2026-10-24 it answered every availability request from our runner host with `HTTP/2 429` (an HTML "429 Too Many Requests" page, no `Retry-After` header).

Code (csharp template `main` at 4cc372e):

- [`checkWaybackMachine`](https://github.com/link-foundation/csharp-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/blob/4cc372ebf2075a0c948b46881143f7d66f0f0e7e/scripts/check-web-archive.mjs#L151-L191)

```js
if (!response.ok) {
  console.warn(`  Wayback API returned ${response.status} for ${url}`);
  return { available: false, archiveUrl: null, timestamp: null };
}
...
} catch (error) {
  console.warn(`  Failed to check Wayback Machine for ${url}: ${error.message}`);
  return { available: false, archiveUrl: null, timestamp: null };
}
```

## Reproduction

`repro-web-archive-429.sh` copies the given `check-web-archive.mjs` into a temporary directory and points its Wayback API at a local fake. The fake answers the first request with 429 and has a snapshot for every URL after that. The script then runs it on a lychee report with one broken link:

```bash
repro-web-archive-429.sh /path/to/template/scripts/check-web-archive.mjs; echo "exit=$?"
```

Output against the template (abridged):

```
Checking: https://example.com/moved-page
  Wayback API returned 429 for https://example.com/moved-page
  ✗ Not found in Web Archive
...
::error title=Broken link - No Web Archive fallback::Broken link detected: https://example.com/moved-page
No archived version was found in the Wayback Machine.
exit=1
== fake Wayback API requests
GET /wayback/available?url=https%3A%2F%2Fexample.com%2Fmoved-page
  -> 429
```

The script needs only `node` and `python3`. It lives in link-foundation/link-cli (issue 134, pull request 135) at `dev/log/issues/134/pulls/135/scripts/repro-web-archive-429.sh`. The outputs are in `dev/log/issues/134/pulls/135/analysis/`:

- `template-web-archive-429-repro.txt`: all three templates;
- `web-archive-429-repro.txt`: link-cli before and after the fix. After one 429 the fixed script retries and reports the snapshot (exit 0); with a persistent 429 it reports `Web Archive lookup failed: HTTP 429 (after 3 attempts)`;
- `web-archive-lookup-429.txt`: the same comparison against the live archive.org 429.

The rust, js and csharp templates share this code; it is reported to each of them.

## Workaround

Re-run the link check later. Before replacing a link, look the URL up on https://web.archive.org/ by hand.

## Suggested fix

1. Retry network errors, 429, 5xx and unreadable JSON with backoff.
2. Return a definite "no snapshot" answer (an empty `archived_snapshots`) at once, without retrying.
3. When the attempts run out, return an `error` and report it as **"Web Archive lookup failed"** with a re-run hint, never as "No archived version".

```js
import { setTimeout as sleep } from 'node:timers/promises';

export async function checkWaybackMachine(
  url,
  { fetchImpl = fetch, attempts = 3, delayMs = 2000, timeoutMs = 10000 } = {}
) {
  const apiUrl = `${WAYBACK_API}${encodeURIComponent(url)}`;
  const notArchived = { available: false, archiveUrl: null, timestamp: null };
  let failure = '';

  for (let attempt = 1; attempt <= attempts; attempt += 1) {
    if (attempt > 1) {
      console.log(`  Retrying the Wayback API (attempt ${attempt} of ${attempts}) after: ${failure}`);
      await sleep(delayMs * 2 ** (attempt - 2));
    }
    let response;
    try {
      response = await fetchImpl(apiUrl, {
        headers: { 'User-Agent': 'broken-link-checker/1.0 (GitHub Actions CI)' },
        signal: AbortSignal.timeout(timeoutMs),
      });
    } catch (error) {
      // fetch() rejects with a bare "fetch failed" and keeps the reason in cause.
      const cause = error.cause?.code ?? error.cause?.message;
      failure = cause ? `${error.message}: ${cause}` : error.message;
      continue;
    }
    if (response.status === 429 || response.status >= 500) {
      failure = `HTTP ${response.status}`;
      continue;
    }
    if (!response.ok) {
      return { ...notArchived, error: `HTTP ${response.status}` };
    }
    let data;
    try {
      data = await response.json();
    } catch (error) {
      failure = `unreadable answer: ${error.message}`;
      continue;
    }
    if (data.archived_snapshots?.closest?.available === true) {
      const snapshot = data.archived_snapshots.closest;
      return {
        available: true,
        archiveUrl: snapshot.url.replace(/^http:\/\//, 'https://'),
        timestamp: snapshot.timestamp,
      };
    }
    return notArchived;
  }
  return { ...notArchived, error: `${failure} (after ${attempts} attempts)` };
}
```

In `main`, collect the results that have an `error` in their own list. Print them as `::error title=Broken link - Web Archive lookup failed::… How to fix: re-run the job. If the lookup keeps failing, fix or remove the link.` and require that list to be empty for `all_archived=true`. Exporting the function with an injectable `fetchImpl` makes it testable without network access. link-cli has these tests in `.github/scripts/check-web-archive.test.mjs`:

- a dropped connection, then a snapshot;
- a persistent failure reports `fetch failed: ECONNRESET (after 2 attempts)`;
- a definite "no snapshot" answer is not retried, and a 403 is reported after one request.

## Summary

`scripts/check-release-needed.mjs` sends **one request** to NuGet and one to the GitHub releases API to decide whether to release. Neither retries. A single dropped connection (Node's fetch throws `TypeError: fetch failed`), a 429 or a 5xx answer fails the release decision, and with it the release job of a push to `main`. Because the error message is the bare `fetch failed`, the network cause (`ECONNRESET`, `ETIMEDOUT`, …) that Node keeps in `error.cause.code` is lost.

Code (template `main` at 4cc372e):

- [`fetchNugetVersions`](https://github.com/link-foundation/csharp-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/blob/4cc372ebf2075a0c948b46881143f7d66f0f0e7e/scripts/check-release-needed.mjs#L142-L158)
- [`fetchGithubReleaseExists`](https://github.com/link-foundation/csharp-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/blob/4cc372ebf2075a0c948b46881143f7d66f0f0e7e/scripts/check-release-needed.mjs#L169-L196)

```js
const response = await fetchImpl(url);
if (response.status === 404) {
  return null;
}
if (!response.ok) {
  throw new Error(`NuGet flat-container index returned HTTP ${response.status} for ${packageId}`);
}
```

Failing is better than guessing here, so the decision is never wrong. But one transient failure turns a good release into a red run.

## Reproduction

`release-needed-transient-fetch.mjs` imports `fetchNugetVersions` from the given file. It passes a fake fetch that throws Node's dropped-connection error once and then answers like nuget.org:

```bash
node release-needed-transient-fetch.mjs /path/to/template/scripts/check-release-needed.mjs; echo "exit=$?"
```

```
Fetching https://api.nuget.org/v3-flatcontainer/example/index.json
FAILED after 1 request(s): fetch failed
exit=1
```

link-foundation/link-cli hit the same class of failure in its dependency check (run 40084225032: `coverlet.collector: fetch failed`). It fixed both scripts in issue 134, pull request 135. The reproduction script and its output are in that pull request:

- `dev/log/issues/134/pulls/135/scripts/release-needed-transient-fetch.mjs`
- `dev/log/issues/134/pulls/135/analysis/release-needed-transient-fetch-repro.txt`

That output also shows the fixed script retrying and succeeding on the second request.

## Workaround

Re-run the failed release job.

## Suggested fix

Wrap both lookups in a small retry helper:

- retry network errors, 429 and 5xx with exponential backoff;
- return any other response at once, so 404 still means "not published";
- name the network cause in the final error.

```js
import { setTimeout as sleep } from 'node:timers/promises';

export async function fetchWithRetries(url, options, fetchImpl = fetch, { attempts = 4, delayMs = 1000 } = {}) {
  for (let attempt = 1; ; attempt += 1) {
    let response;
    try {
      response = await fetchImpl(url, options);
    } catch (error) {
      if (attempt >= attempts) {
        const cause = error.cause ? `: ${error.cause.code ?? error.cause.message ?? error.cause}` : '';
        throw new Error(`${url}: ${error.message}${cause} (after ${attempt} attempts)`);
      }
    }
    const transient = !response || response.status === 429 || response.status >= 500;
    if (!transient || attempt >= attempts) {
      return response;
    }
    console.log(`Retrying ${url} (attempt ${attempt + 1} of ${attempts})`);
    await sleep(delayMs * 2 ** (attempt - 1));
  }
}
```

`fetchNugetVersions(packageId, fetchImpl = fetch, retry = {})` and `fetchGithubReleaseExists(repository, tag, fetchImpl = fetch, retry = {})` then call `fetchWithRetries(url, options, fetchImpl, retry)`. Passing `{ delayMs: 0 }` keeps the tests fast. Tests worth adding:

- a dropped connection, then success;
- exhausted attempts report `fetch failed: ETIMEDOUT (after 2 attempts)`;
- a 404 is not retried, while a 429 is.

link-cli has these tests in `csharp/scripts/release-scripts.test.mjs`.
